CommitDate: Fri Sep 11 16:46:48 2026 +0530

    fix(search): derive the abbreviation table from identifier frequencies

    The hand-written table carried 74 pairs and lost a real query to one of
    them: "return 4xx on invalid input for delete method" (SWE-bench
    caddyserver__caddy-5870) dropped its gold file out of the top ten,
    because delete -> del pulled every Del() header helper into the head.
    Verified by removal: deleting that one entry restored the gold file to
    rank 6, exactly matching the pre-table build.

    The failure class, not the failure row: an alias earns its place only
    when the query genuinely needs help and the alias genuinely reaches
    something. Keep rule, measured over 1.4M identifier tokens from 40 repos
    (21 SWE-bench Multilingual repos across nine languages, 18 pinned
    popular Go repos, entireio/cli):

      - the LONG form occurs at most 50 per 100k identifier tokens. A word
        code already writes needs no alias, and the alias only adds noise.
        This clause catches delete (80/100k), context (139), error (345),
        source (683).
      - the ALIAS occurs at least 25 times. It must reach something real:
        txn occurs ONCE in 1.4M tokens, authz seven times, sess zero.

    23 of 74 pairs failed; 51 kept. Deliberately NOT a code-prefers-the-
    short-form ratio test: an earlier draft used one and wrongly pruned
    database -> db (code writes "database" in symbol names 3:1 over "db",
    yet openDB-style names are real and reachable only through the alias --
    pinned by TestSearchReviewRoundFourRegressions).

    Corpus effect (53 tuning + 30 sealed held-out SWE-bench instances, gold
    = files touched by the human fix): the caddy regression is fixed at main
    parity, recall@10 flat on both sets and both query forms, recall@5 +-1
    instance per set, MRR within 0.015 of main with every residual loss
    diagnosed (two rank-1s that were riding the substring scorer's habit of
    counting one query word's morphological variants as separate coverage
    terms, one alias-adjacent shuffle on a documentation query).

    All cli case studies hold: "authentication" 36.7 with an all-auth head,
    the headline login query, plural configurations, repositories.

    Refs #247.

    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Entire-Checkpoint: 66bd084c0072

