{"body":"## Correction: two claims in the description were wrong\n\n@ashtom pushed `1bd66098`, `51a1628e` and `f52f6088` fixing two real defects in the first four commits. Recording the corrections visibly rather than only editing the description, since the description asserted one of these as fixed when it was not.\n\n### 1. The determiner cue did not fix the false positive, it fixed one instance of it\n\nThe description for `2923d52e` claimed a determiner before the head was the part-of-speech cue that keeps a logging query from becoming a login query. It catches `write **the log** in json`. It does nothing when `log` is genuinely a verb and its object sits between verb and particle:\n\n```\nquery: \"log a message in json format\"          (before f52f6088)\n  1  29.6  newLoginCmd\n  2  29.1  RecordLoginContext\n```\n\nThe exact false positive the commit message declared fixed, still present in a form I did not test. I verified the one example I had written the guard from, then generalised from it.\n\n`searchCompoundFormatPreposition` is the actual fix: reject the join when `in` introduces an output format (`json`, `yaml`, …, or any word followed by `format`/`encoding`) rather than completing a phrasal verb. After it, the same query returns `log` and `formatMessageSuffix`.\n\nThe scoping across `51a1628e` and `f52f6088` is the part worth reading. The guard applies to `login` only on the adjacent path, and to every verb on the separated path, because the two shapes put the object in different places: separated, the object already precedes `in`, so a trailing format describes *that object* for any verb (\"sign the request in JSON\"); adjacent, \"check in a JSON file\" puts its object after `in` and must still join. The tests pin both directions, including that `log the user in and return JSON` still recovers login.\n\n### 2. Plural queries never reached the alias table at all\n\nA defect in `e3fec8ea` and `aa545311` (now folded into `e3fec8ea`). The retrieval loop iterated `originalTerms`, a snapshot taken **before** the morphological expansion ran, and then did an exact `searchTermAbbreviations[term]` lookup. A plural long form therefore never matched a table key. `searchNameMatchesAbbreviation` had the identical exact-key bug, so plurals missed at both retrieval and scoring.\n\n| query | before | after |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `configurations` | 10.4, `Configured` then noise | **26.5** `LoadCheckpointsConfig`, `Config`, `globalConfigDirPath` |\n| `repositories` | 35.4 | **41.1**, same hits, alias now firing |\n\n`configurations` was returning essentially nothing while `configuration` worked, which is the failure mode the abbreviation work existed to remove, reintroduced one inflection over. `searchAbbreviations()` expands word variants before the lookup and the snapshot moved to after morphology, which also reaches `-ies`: repositories → repository → repo.\n\n### Why the eval missed both\n\nThe fixture eval added in `7de5a305` passed throughout. Its cases were written from the same examples that drove the code, so it could only confirm what I had already checked by hand. The new cases — `plural configuration reaches config`, `log a message in json format`, `check in a JSON file`, `sign the request in json format` — are the queries I did not think to write.\n\nThat is an argument for the corpus-scale ranking suite discussed in #247 that has nothing to do with regression gating: its value is supplying queries no one on the team would have invented. SWE-bench `problem_statement` text gives exactly that, written by people who did not know the codebase.\n\n### Status\n\nFull `./internal/...` green at `f52f6088`, exit 0. Eval now six cases, all passing. GraphMark Fidelity and Tasks still show zero metric differences against `main`.\n","created_at":"2026-09-11T03:42:01Z","user":"karthik-rameshkumar"}
{"body":"## Corpus study: 83 SWE-bench instances, sealed held-out, per-mechanism attribution\n\nThe fixture eval missed two real bugs (see the @ashtom corrections above), so this branch was put through the measurement it lacked: gold-file retrieval over real SWE-bench Multilingual issue text, gold = files touched by the human fix, two query forms (issue title, first two sentences), nine languages. 53-instance tuning set (`agentic-swebench/.tune_recall.json`), then a **30-instance held-out slice hash-selected with a recorded seed before any held-out result was seen**.\n\n### Headline numbers (gold-file recall, `main` vs this branch at `1c466155`)\n\n| set | form | metric | main | branch |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| tuning 53 | title | recall@5 / @10 | 52.8% / 66.0% | **54.7%** / 66.0% |\n| tuning 53 | title | MRR | 0.3925 | 0.3890 |\n| tuning 53 | first2 | recall@5 / @10 | 56.6% / 60.4% | 56.6% / 60.4% |\n| held-out 30 | title | hit@5 / @10 | 23/30 / 24/30 | 23/30 / 24/30 |\n| held-out 30 | title | MRR | 0.5575 | 0.5409 |\n| held-out 30 | first2 | hit@5 / @10 | 24/30 / 24/30 | **23/30** / 24/30 |\…423 tokens truncated…3→2, php-cs-fixer 4→3, prometheus 4→3).\n\n**3. One held-out loss belongs to the alias mechanism itself and is accepted:** fmt-2317 (4→6, first2), where the query genuinely discusses documentation and `documentation → doc` boosts doc-adjacent files. The entry survives the frequency rule and earns its keep elsewhere.\n\n### Also found: a measurement bug in the existing recall harness\n\n`tools/recall_agent_query.py` runs `--format text --max-context-bytes 3000`; at `--top-k 10` that emits **4** ranked paths (json/unbounded emits 10, measured on jq-2235). Its published \"recall @ top-5\" was recall over whatever survived the payload byte budget. This study used json/unbounded. Will be reported to the benchmark repo separately.\n\n### Where this leaves the merge\n\nThe gate proposed earlier was \"corpus flat-or-better\". The strict reading fails on MRR by 0.004-0.015, entirely explained by two accidental rank-1s ending and one alias-adjacent shuffle. The case for merging anyway: the defect this PR exists to fix (prose vocabulary never reaching code spelling) is measured and large where it applies (`authentication` 16.6 → 36.7 on entireio/cli, above the 34.6 of the code spelling itself; the headline login query fixed; plurals fixed), the SWE-bench corpus is dominated by queries already written in code vocabulary where these features correctly stay out of the way, and every negative delta now has a name and a mechanism attached.\n","created_at":"2026-09-11T10:25:54Z","user":"karthik-rameshkumar"}
{"body":"## Re-measured on the current head (`3016b04e`): recall flat, first2 MRR positive, every delta named\n\nThe corpus study two comments up measured a superseded build; the branch has since gained nine commits of alias-mechanics hardening plus the frequency-derived table. Re-ran the 53-instance tuning set against `main` on the actual head:\n\n| form | metric | main | head `3016b04e` |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| title | recall@5 / @10 | 52.8% / 66.0% | 52.8% / 66.0% |\n| title | MRR | 0.3925 | 0.3853 |\n| first2 | recall@5 / @10 | 56.6% / 60.4% | 56.6% / 60.4% |\n| first2 | MRR | 0.4253 | **0.4260** |\n\nGold-rank movements, all of them: **8 improvements** (laravel 2→1, nushell 3→2, jq 6→5, axios 8→6, immutable-js 3→2, fmt-3248 3→2, vue-11739 8→6 and 3→2) vs **2 regressions**: fluentd 5→6, and vue-11899 1→5, which is the diagnosed substring-variant-multi-counting artifact ending (see the corpus-study comment). The caddy delete-method regression is gone: the frequency-derived table no longer carries `delete → del`.\n\n### Table criterion revised after review feedback\n\nThe first derivation used a code-prefers-the-short-form ratio and wrongly pruned `database → db`; `TestSearchReviewRoundFourRegressions` caught it (`openDB` must be reachable from a \"database\" query, and 183 real `openDB`-style tokens exist). Final rule, measured over 1.4M identifier tokens from 40 repos: **long form ≤ 50 per 100k** (catches every entry with measured harm: delete 80, context 139, error 345, source 683) **AND alias ≥ 25 occurrences** (kills the dead aliases: txn appears once in 1.4M tokens, authz seven times, sess zero). 51 of 74 pairs survive. The derivation and the do-not-add-by-taste rule are in the table's doc comment.\n\n### One open thread: perf\n\nOn a cold cache against `entireio/cli`, this head ran the `authentication` query in 155s where the pre-rebase build took 113s, both measured under corpus-run contention, so neither number is clean. Worth one quiet-machine timing of the boundary-aware git scan before merge; filed as a review question, not a blocker claim.\n\nFull `./internal/...` green at `3016b04e`, including the round-four regression tests and the fixture eval.\n","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:39:00Z","user":"karthik-rameshkumar"}
